\paragraph{Filler composition.}
\textbf{Code-heavy business filler produced lower accuracy than unrelated prose.} The matched difference was \qualityTwoRelevantChange{} points [\qualityTwoRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityTwoRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the shortest tier and \qualityFourRelevantChange{} points [\qualityFourRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityFourRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the middle tier. At the shortest tier, the paired outcomes were \qualityTwoRelevantWins{} win, \qualityTwoRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityTwoRelevantTies{} ties. At the middle tier, they were \qualityFourRelevantWins{} win, \qualityFourRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityFourRelevantTies{} ties. The longest-tier estimate was \qualityEightRelevantChange{} points, but its \qualityEightRelevantFamilyCount{} families are too sparse to strengthen that conclusion. Because the business filler also adds code-shaped candidates and repeated entity templates, these results do not show that topical relevance caused the difference.

\paragraph{Mitigation.}
\textbf{The query-entity selector reversed the keyword filter's failure.} Full-context accuracy was \pruningFullAccuracy{}\%, compared with \pruningPrunedAccuracy{}\% after keyword filtering and \entitySelectorAccuracy{}\% after query-entity selection. Query-entity selection improved on full context by \entitySelectorChange{} points [\entitySelectorChangeLow{}, \entitySelectorChangeHigh{}], with \entitySelectorWins{} wins and no losses. Compared with keyword pruning, it gained \selectorComparisonChange{} points [\selectorComparisonChangeLow{}, \selectorComparisonChangeHigh{}], with \selectorComparisonWins{} wins, no losses, and \selectorComparisonTies{} ties (Table~\ref{tab:pruning}).

