65:\paragraph{Model and execution.}
70:\paragraph{Scenario families.}
71:For each family, seeded code generates the target company and code, a competing company and code, a question, and filler. The question asks for the renewal code needed to extend the target company's contract. Its wording exactly matches the competing clause, whereas the answer-bearing clause calls the same value a renewal identifier. A matched literal control reverses the wording of the two clauses while retaining the same filler.
73:For each full-context family, we insert the answer clause near the start, middle, or end. Family generation does not use target position in any random seed. We assign the competing clause a fractional insertion index, either earlier or later in the document, that is independent of answer position and fixed across the three versions. Its final sentence index shifts by one when the answer is inserted before it. Thus, each paired position contrast changes both the answer placement and this mechanical one-sentence shift while preserving the scenario content and filler order.
75:\paragraph{Amount and filler control.}
76:The tokenizer, rather than a character estimate, determines prompt length. The three full-context tiers average \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Each tier balances business filler with unrelated sentences. Business filler consists of other companies, records, and unrelated codes; unrelated filler describes places and events outside the contract domain. Filler pairs share the family, target position, question, and measured tier.
78:This manipulation changes several properties together. Compared with unrelated prose, the related filler contains more business entities, contract-like templates, and code-shaped answer candidates. The paired comparison therefore identifies the effect of these two generated filler conditions, not the effect of topical relevance alone.
80:The final tier contains fewer families because it is more expensive to run. Prompt-length estimates therefore use only the \amountSharedEightCount{} cases shared by every tier rather than the unequal full-tier totals. We balance both filler bundles within each reported position and length comparison but do not interpret their contrast as a context-quality effect.
82:\paragraph{Selectors and controls.}
87:\section{Scoring and Comparisons}
91:The inferential target is the population of synthetic scenario families defined by the seeded generator. To compute each \confidenceLevel{}\% interval, we resample whole observed families while retaining all positions, filler types, and variants from every selected family. Exact enumeration makes this cluster bootstrap deterministic. Paired changes are differences in accuracy over an exact intersection of scenario keys, and their intervals use the same family-level resampling. The intervals describe sensitivity to the small sample of families, while wins, losses, and ties expose the underlying paired outcomes. Because greedy decoding is deterministic, the intervals do not measure uncertainty from repeated decoding.
93:\section{Results}
95:\paragraph{Answer to the first question: position.}
125:\paragraph{Answer to the second question: lexical selection.}
166:\paragraph{Answer to the third question: prompt length.}
192:\section{Limitations}
194:The findings concern one small quantized model and may not transfer to larger models or other quantization levels. Synthetic clause lookup is not natural contract analysis, and retrieval accuracy is not a measure of general capability. The \cpuBudgetMinutes{}-minute CPU budget capped the grid, leaving only \tierEightCount{} full-context probes at the longest tier and wide intervals throughout. That sparse tier prevents a conclusion about degradation within the measured range, and the experiment cannot address longer prompts. The filler comparison confounds topical relation with semantic domain, entity and code density, candidate count, and sentence templates. A factorial design that matches those properties would be needed to identify a topical-relevance effect. Greedy decoding removes run-to-run sampling variation but does not show how sampling would change the estimates. Query-entity selection assumes that the question names one company exactly and that the relevant sentence repeats that name. It does not address aliases, pronouns, or queries without a unique named subject. The decoy-removal control also depends on an oracle label unavailable in natural documents.
196:\section{Reproducibility}
